PN,31601
SC,0001
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F316A1785633476
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FA drifting SpaceX rocket is set to
OL,7,Fcrash into the moon on Wednesday,
OL,8,Ftracking experts say.
OL,10,FThe rocket is on a collision course
OL,11,Fwith the moon after it launched a pair
OL,12,Fof lunar landers more than a year ago.
OL,14,FIts upper stage will unintentionally
OL,15,Fslam into Earth's only natural
OL,16,Fsatellite, carving out a crater and
OL,17,Fsending up a plume of dust and rubble.
OL,19,FThe upper stage is the final part of
OL,20,Fthe rocket that ignites, propelling it
OL,21,Finto space.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G1/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,317,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31602
SC,0002
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F316A1785633476
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FScientists and skygazers are eager to
OL,7,Fobserve the phenomenon, which will
OL,8,Foccur in the early hours, meaning
OL,9,Feastern areas in the U.S. and Canada,
OL,10,Fand much of South America should have
OL,11,Fthe best views.
OL,13,FThe space craft is predicted to hit
OL,14,Fnear the Einstein Crater at an impact
OL,15,Fof 5,400 mph (8,700 kph), seven times
OL,16,Fthe speed of sound, and packing the
OL,17,Fequivalent energy of three tons of
OL,18,FTNT, according to Space tracking
OL,19,Fexpert Bill Gray.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G2/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,317,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31603
SC,0003
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F316A1785633476
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FAlthough scientists are not overly
OL,7,Fconcerned with the space junk's
OL,8,Fmovements, it highlights the growing
OL,9,Fthreat as more and more items cram
OL,10,Finto orbit.
OL,12,F"Things are getting crowded up there,"
OL,13,Fsaid Gray, who plans to view the
OL,14,Faftermath from New Brunswick, Canada.
OL,16,FSpaceX never meant to hit the moon,
OL,17,Fbut experts suggest it could have been
OL,18,Favoided if that part of the rocket had
OL,19,Fbeen nudged into orbit around the sun.
OL,26,@tD@AOUtD@fO]
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G3/9
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
